e  going to suffer severely because of this  action  you've written that africa needs  gas power and and plants gas power  plants to provide electricity tell us a  little bit more about why  so if you're building a robust  electricity sector  you cannot just build it based on  solar and wind  and if we say that african countries  have to develop just based on renewable  energy and many of these countries do  not have  hydro  and geothermal then what are you left  with you're left with gas as a  transitional  fuel source  and this is not unusual to africa just  this week just this week  the  german chancellor in in in waiting all  of schultz  was categorical that germany will have  to install  new additional gas-fired plants if  they're supposed to if if they're to  transition to higher shares of renewable  energy so if germany that's very  sophisticated advanced you know with all  the technological know-how and the  resources has to build gas power plants  how much more poorer countries that are  just developing the electricity sector  all right and that's where we'll leave  it  is an energy sector expert in nairobi  thank you for your time and your insight  now talking of bringing power to the  millions of africans who are without  let's meet one farmer in eastern rwanda  who was tired of sitting in the dark he  brought electricity to his village and  managed to make it climate friendly at  the same time  israel hibimana was fed up living  without electricity  so he decided to take matters into his  own hands  he built a hydropower plant after  selling two of his houses to buy used  engines and water pipes in kigali  [Music]  i never went to school  the idea for it came to me when i saw a  small hydroelectric plant when i was a  child  that's why when i grew up  i wanted a similar plant  so he could generate electricity in my  village  the plant initially supplied more than  50 families in his village with  electricity  but his first model failed to regulate  the electricity supply and the surges  burned out people's electronics  israel didn't give up the plant was  modernized and expanded with the help of  the rwanda energy group it now  gives power to 170 households  and hundreds of others are in the  process of getting electricity  and light  for the first time  what a pleasure to have electricity  we're finally done with the darkness yes  this schoolgirl is delighted she won't  fall behind in her homework  only one traditional lamp and didn't  have enough food for everyone to use it  at night this stopped me from studying  now i will be able to study longer even  at night time i can perform well at  school  finally having electricity in the  village is also good for business it  enabled this seamstress to double her  output  i used to use manual sewing machines and  that was really difficult  but after electricity came  i bought electric sewing machines  straight away and that's made my life  much easier  many other opportunities have been  created in this village since the  arrival of electricity  thanks to habimana and his hydroelectric  plant  now kumi nadu the environmental activist  is still with me sakumi we've just seen  a very good example there for one energy  solution in a village in rwanda but if  we consider the scale uh to which we  need to electrify africa fossil fuels do  have a record um some voices on the  continent say it's unfair to to limit  emissions on the world's poorest  countries  when emissions are rising in  industrialized countries what do you say  to that well it's unfair sure but  poor nations who are paying now the  first and most brutal impacts of climate  change cannot bury their heads in the  sign sand and say just because they did  a bad thing in the rich countries we  also have a right to continue to do bad  to do a bad thing and let's just be  clear africa is not a poor continent  right africa is the richest continent  underneath the ground and that's  precisely why we're one of the poorest  continent above the ground what is  called the resource curse and let's be  clear that example  from rwanda is the example that should  move us  because a lot of energy that's generated  in big plants and then has to be  transmitted long distances to get to the  villages and so on gets lost in the  process and let's be very clear  most of our governments on the continent  know  that we have huge solar potential huge  wind potential huge wave potential right  because we are a continent right  surrounded by  and on the argument that  it it's uh you know the sun blows it uh  shines at some time and the wind blows  that that problem is being fixed by  advances in battery storage capability  and i would say and i challenge any  african leader to tell me otherwise  right that the reason most governments  around the world are so committed to big  fossil fuel projects at the moment is  because the supply chain of corruption  right is deeper bigger and juicier i'm  not saying that renewable energy is  immune from corruption  but it is much less and so if and and  and if we do not have a decentralized  approach right  rural areas in throughout our continent  we can turn it around very quickly with  small micro solar grids  with the kind of example that we saw  with micro hydro and so on so  i don't buy the argument that many of  our governments make that we have to do  the dirty stuff even though the dirty  stuff is killing us right now and the  unfairness of it is  recognized right and that's why we push  so hard to have this 100 billion a year  in the green climate fund to outpour  countries i have 10 seconds left on the  clock what commitment do you want to see  from africa's leaders going into the  summit  we want them to say  we will take  climate change seriously we will  prioritize the poor in the way we  allocate resources we want advantage  industry at the expense of the people  and that they will put people before  profit and before corruption all right  that